666810389,2026-10-18 23:35:12,macrossluvsrobotech,2019-07-30,1018,2,Bearish,"$CBRS Think Cerebras ($CBRS) has an untouchable &quot;wafer moat&quot;? Don’t buy the bull—it’s an expensive illusion. The massive lies of $CBRS ignore the physical constraints of frontier models. Yes, the CS-3 has incredible on-wafer speed, but it has only 44GB of SRAM. Trillion-parameter frontier models require terabytes. The &quot;only 44GB&quot; limit means the rest of the model must be siphoned off-wafer to external DRAM appliances (MemoryX) over cables. It&#39;s a lie that Cerebras is a self-contained savior—it’s heavily memory-buffered, and once the data leaves the wafer, it hits the exact slow bottlenecks it claims to solve, just at a much higher cost. $CBRS is a resource-intensive, raw-material nightmare that is hard to scale. It’s why Sam Altman/OpenAI turned to &quot;Jalapeño&quot; (their custom Broadcom inference chip), and the industry is sticking with NVIDIA&#39;s massively adaptable Blackwell ecosystem. High cost, low scalability, and 44GB of capacity make the &quot;moat&quot; a bottleneck. 📉🚨 $NVDA $SPY $USO"
